If I change this part to:

```c
		if (ppp_lcp) {
			ppp_lcp->close(ctx, "Shutdown");
			/* signaling for the carrier off event is done from the LCP callback */
		}
		ppp_change_phase(ctx, PPP_DEAD);

		ppp_mgmt_raise_carrier_off_event(ctx->iface);
		net_if_carrier_down(ctx->iface);
```

then the modem is able to start during the second cycle.

I added `NET_WARN("lcp_finished");` to `lcp_finished` but the line is not printed, maybe `lcp_finished` isn't invoked for some reason?

Indeed I turned off my modem too quickly, as sometimes it doesn't reply to the shutdown command/restart itself after shut down.

I don't really have an example, but a simple way to test it:

```c
/*  pseudo code */
while (true) {
        enable_modem_power();
        gsm_ppp_start();
        k_msleep(60000);
        gsm_ppp_stop();
        disable_modem_power();
        k_msleep(20000);
}

Just looking at the gsm_ppp_stop function, I think to make it work properly a semaphore construction should be between, to wait for the interface to be properly closed off (NET_EVENT_IF_DOWN) event:

`net_if_l2(iface)->enable(iface, false);`

and 

```
if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_GSM_MUX)) {
		/* Lower mux_enabled flag to trigger re-sending AT+CMUX etc */
		gsm->mux_enabled = false;

		if (gsm->ppp_dev) {
			uart_mux_disable(gsm->ppp_dev);
		}
	}
```

The ->enable(iface, false) triggers the close, but the gsm->mux_enabled = false; needs to be delayed for the FSM to finish its termination.

This construction introduces a dependency between the GSM_PPP driver and the NET_MGMT_EVENT. But on the other hand it enables someone to use the GSM_PPP driver out of the box.
An alternative is to poll for the interface to go down.
